        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/10931297?u=600cfcb4c4e709dfbc66b328147d846ee87b1e51&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/>\n\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Description</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">There's two Resource Timing docs:</p>\n<ul dir=\"auto\">\n<li><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Performance_API/Resource_timing\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Performance_API/Resource_timing</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/PerformanceResourceTiming\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/PerformanceResourceTiming</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p dir=\"auto\">And timings are duplicated in both. We've added some new timings to this but looks like I only added them to the latter in previous PRs (<a class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"1851325981\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/28569\" data-hovercard-type=\"pull_request\" data-hovercard-url=\"/mdn/content/pull/28569/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/28569\">#28569</a> and <a class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"2802324227\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/37740\" data-hovercard-type=\"pull_request\" data-hovercard-url=\"/mdn/content/pull/37740/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/37740\">#37740</a>)</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Motivation</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">Fix inconsistencies.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">We maybe should consider if it's really that useful to duplicate this information in two different places as I'm sure this is bound to happen again.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Additional details</h3>\n\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Related issues and pull requests</h3>\n\n\n\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">Previous PRs:</p>\n<ul dir=\"auto\">\n<li><a class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"1851325981\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/28569\" data-hovercard-type=\"pull_request\" data-hovercard-url=\"/mdn/content/pull/28569/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/28569\">#28569</a></li>\n<li><a class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"2802324227\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/37740\" data-hovercard-type=\"pull_request\" data-hovercard-url=\"/mdn/content/pull/37740/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/37740\">#37740</a></li>\n</ul>",
            "url": "https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41373",
            "title": "Add missing interim/final timings to Resource Timing doc",
            "date_modified": "2025-10-02T16:00:31.000Z",
            "date_published": "2025-10-02T15:58:22.000Z",
            "author": {
                "name": "tunetheweb",
                "url": "https://github.com/tunetheweb"
            }
        },
        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/10931297?u=600cfcb4c4e709dfbc66b328147d846ee87b1e51&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/>\n\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Description</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\"><a href=\"https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/performance/extension\" rel=\"nofollow\">Chrome DevTool's extensibility API</a> extends some standard (and non-standard!) Web APIs with either new additions, or in the use of structured versions of existing fields.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Therefore I think it is helpful for developers who may see these being used in the wild to understand them.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">At the same time, as these are non-standard extensions (at least for now!) I don't think it should be documented on MDN other than a small note. I'm also conscious that MDN is not a documentation site for specific browsers, but rather the web platform which is another reason to keep this light. Happy to take feedback on this and iterate if you don't think I've struck the right balance here.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Motivation</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">A developer approached me confused that the <code class=\"notranslate\">console.timeStamp</code> MDN docs suggested this API did not allow for the extensions Chrome uses.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Additionally, with the likes of popular libraries like <a href=\"https://react.dev/reference/dev-tools/react-performance-tracks\" rel=\"nofollow\">React</a> and <a href=\"https://angular.dev/best-practices/profiling-with-chrome-devtools\" rel=\"nofollow\">Angular</a> uses the extensibility API, this is becoming a more popular API.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Firefox have also expressed interest to potentially implementing these extensions (at which point we can document this more on MDN as it's more cross-browser).</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Additional details</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\"><a href=\"https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/performance/extension\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/performance/extension</a></p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Related issues and pull requests</h3>\n\n\n\n",
            "url": "https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41372",
            "title": "Add information about Chrome DevTools extensibility API",
            "date_modified": "2025-10-02T14:17:42.000Z",
            "date_published": "2025-10-02T14:15:22.000Z",
            "author": {
                "name": "tunetheweb",
                "url": "https://github.com/tunetheweb"
            }
        },
        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3654180?u=c5206071d58424c0a084196578278af814fd3817&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/><h3 dir=\"auto\">Description</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Add 2 articles about font smoothing.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Motivation</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">These two articles helped me decide whether font smoothing is still relevant. Not only they are more recent than the other articles linked right now, but they also offer a different perspective.</p>",
